PRIVATE REFLECTION: {Round 3 leaves 12:9 split (B vs C) intact with no new empirical data or resolution. B bloc (Colleagues 1,10,11,12,15,18,19,20,3,4,6,8) continues to stress transcript geometry: poly(dT)+75×225 remains strictly 3′-biased; CDR3 (400–600 nt upstream) lies beyond Read 2 reach on any 3′-anchored molecule. Only TSO insertion after UMI converts to 5′-RACE/template-switching, placing CDR3 inside the 225 bp read while preserving bead barcoding/UMI, Abseq/Sample-Tag/mRNA compatibility, Rhapsody workflow, and fixed sequencing config; BD commercial immune-repertoire module already uses this placement. Minor open variable: routine TSO-density titration on mixed beads.

C bloc (13,14,16,17,2,5,7,9) reiterates literal “same bead schematic” constraint and claims flanking TCR oligos (upstream V/leader + downstream constant) physically anchor the full V(D)J junction at hybridization so the existing 225 bp read traverses CDR3; they assert this is a routine mixed-oligo titration already supported by BD Rhapsody and avoids altering validated oligo architecture or introducing unvalidated template-switching on low-abundance TCR transcripts.

No factual rebuttal to B’s positional argument has emerged; C-style flanking capture still leaves molecules 3′-anchored from poly(dT), so the distance problem persists unless the flanking oligos fully supplant poly(dT) capture for TCR transcripts (which is not stated). Prior assessment unchanged—B remains the single minimal, architecture-correct solution satisfying every explicit constraint; C cannot overcome the read-start geometry without altering read length/orientation or the bead schematic. Persistent disagreement centers on whether C’s physical-anchoring claim actually repositions sequencing start relative to CDR3.}

